      <p>Amsterdam's mean availability of just 92.5 days/year is consistent with its <strong>30-night/year cap</strong> on short-term rentals in many districts. This is the clearest regulatory fingerprint in the dataset. Barcelona's extreme reading — <strong>40.1% of listings require a 30+ night minimum</strong> — is a different regulatory adaptation: hosts setting long minimum stays to reclassify properties as medium-term rentals, partially circumventing Airbnb-specific restrictions. Paris's anomalously high mean minimum nights (43.9 days vs. a median of 3) is driven by a small number of listings with extreme values (up to 1,000 nights), suggesting data quality issues or deliberate blocking strategies.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════ SECTION 6: KEY TAKEAWAYS ═══════════════════ -->
  <section class="section" id="s6">
    <div class="section-label">Section 06</div>
    <div class="section-title">Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</div>
    <p class="section-desc">Data-derived findings for investors, market entrants, and policymakers. All conclusions are drawn directly from the dataset.</p>

    <div class="card" style="padding:0; overflow:hidden;">
      <ul class="takeaway-list">
        <li><strong>Amsterdam is the highest-priced market (in USD-comparable terms) with the most regulated supply.</strong> At a median of $222/night and only 92.5 mean availability days/year, Amsterdam combines premium pricing with severe supply constraint — a direct result of its 30-night/year cap. For investors, this creates both opportunity (pricing power) and risk (volume limits). For policymakers, it's the clearest example in this dataset of regulation successfully compressing short-term rental availability.</li>

        <li><strong>Bangkok and Barcelona are most dominated by professional operators — over 58% of listings are held by hosts with 5+ properties.</strong> This signals that both markets have already transitioned from a peer-to-peer sharing model toward a commercial property management model. Regulatory bodies in both cities face a concentrated operator base; Barcelona's 40.1% min-30-night share suggests regulation is already forcing adaptation tactics by these operators.</li>

        <li><strong>Rome delivers the strongest guest demand signals of any city in the dataset.</strong> With the highest average reviews/month (1.77), highest LTM review count (14.35/listing), and 73.4% active listing rate, Rome shows sustained, broad-based booking demand. Combined with relatively modest pricing (median $131) and low minimum-night requirements, Rome appears accessible and high-turnover — attractive for operators seeking volume over margin.</li>

        <li><strong>Paris and Sydney have no price data in this extract — a critical gap for cross-market investment decisions.</strong> Analysts relying on this dataset cannot benchmark Paris or Sydney pricing. Given that Paris has 13,927 listings (27.9% of the dataset), this absence materially limits the report's pricing conclusions. Any investment or competitive analysis involving these cities must be supplemented with additional data sources.</li>

        <li><strong>London's market is large but shows signs of demand softness relative to its size.</strong> With 16,674 listings — 33% of the dataset — London has only a 50.3% active listing rate and 0.99 average reviews/month, below Rome, Sydney, and Barcelona. This divergence between supply scale and per-listing demand intensity suggests a surplus of inventory relative to actively booked demand, potentially compressing yields for average operators.</li>

        <li><strong>Bangkok prices are denominated in Thai Baht, not USD.</strong> The median of 1,400 THB (~$42 USD at current exchange rates) places Bangkok as a value market when correctly interpreted — not the extreme-high-price outlier it appears in raw data. The high professional host concentration (60.4%) and high availability (252.5 days/year) together indicate a lightly regulated, commercially active market with strong operator infrastructure but lower per-night revenue in USD terms.</li>

        <li><strong>Amsterdam's market profile is an anomaly that should not be generalized.</strong> Its combination of the lowest listings/host ratio (1.06), lowest professional host share (6.8%), lowest max portfolio (35 listings), lowest availability (92.5 days), and highest pricing creates a market that is structurally distinct from all other cities — shaped almost entirely by regulation rather than natural market dynamics. It is not a replicable model without equivalent regulatory infrastructure.</li>
      </ul>
